//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// The negative direction of #324: an indexed element does not silently widen to
// the annotated type. Under the Rust numeric model (#240) there are no implicit
// scalar conversions, so binding an `f16` element to an `f32` is an error and
// the programmer writes `as f32`.
//
// This is the direction worth pinning. The bug being fixed was that indexing
// *always* produced `f32`, and the obvious repair -- widen the element to
// whatever the context asks for -- would make every case in
// tests/backend/pass/tensor_element_type.vx pass while restoring exactly the
// silent retyping that hid the defect for so long. A test that only checks the
// element type arrives correctly cannot tell those two implementations apart;
// this one can.
//
// f32 is deliberately the annotation here, because f32 is where the original
// bug was invisible: if the element type is ever defaulted again, this is the
// case that stays quiet.
//
// RUN: not vxc %s 2>&1 | FileCheck %s
// CHECK: Type mismatch in variable declaration
fn main() -> i32 {
  let mut h : Tensor<f16> = Tensor<f16>([ 2, 2 ]);
  h[0][0] = 1.5;
  let widened : f32 = h[0][0];
  print(widened);
  return 0;
}
